Your response uses camelCase (`intervalDays`, `lastWateredAt`) and the array is returned bare. The SQL below uses the snake_case column names from the table (`interval_days`, `last_watered_at`) and maps them to the same camelCase keys your component reads. Adjust the column name if your table uses something different.

**Backend: `GET /api/plants`**

```js
// app.js
const SORTS = {
  next_watering: `
    ORDER BY
      CASE WHEN p.last_watered_at IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END,
      next_watering_at ASC NULLS LAST,
      p.name ASC`,
  name: `ORDER BY p.name ASC`,
};

app.get('/api/plants', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const sort = req.query.sort;
    const orderBy = SORTS[sort] ?? SORTS.name; // allowlist: never interpolate raw input

    const { rows } = await db.query(
      `SELECT p.id,
              p.name,
              p.interval_days,
              p.last_watered_at,
              p.photo_url,
              CASE WHEN p.last_watered_at IS NULL THEN NULL
                   ELSE p.last_watered_at + make_interval(days => p.interval_days)
              END AS next_watering_at
         FROM plants p
        ${orderBy}`
    );

    res.json(rows.map((r) => ({
      id: r.id,
      name: r.name,
      intervalDays: r.interval_days,
      lastWateredAt: r.last_watered_at ? new Date(r.last_watered_at).toISOString() : null,
      nextWateringAt: r.next_watering_at ? new Date(r.next_watering_at).toISOString() : null,
      photoUrl: r.photo_url,
    })));
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);
  }
});
```

Notes on the SQL:
- `make_interval(days => ...)` is a Postgres function, so the interval stays exact even with fractional-free integer days. `interval_days` is an integer in our schema, so this is safe.
- The sort key is looked up in an allowlist (`SORTS`), so the query parameter is never put into SQL directly.
- `next_watering_at` is `NULL` for never-watered plants, which the `ORDER BY` puts last.

